course: politics
lesson: 4
title: Parties: teams with ideas
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A political party is like a team built around ideas: people who think alike join together, choose candidates and write a plan of promises called a manifesto, and then try to win elections to carry it out.
explain: In the UK the two biggest parties for a long time have been Labour and the Conservatives; in the USA they are the Democrats (whose cartoon symbol is a donkey) and the Republicans (an elephant).
explain: "Left" and "right" are rough labels, nothing to do with which hand you write with: the left often wants the government to do more and share more, the right often wants lower taxes, smaller government and to keep traditions, and most people are a mix.
explain: Smaller parties bring their own big topics, like the Greens with the environment or the SNP with Scotland running itself, and some candidates belong to no party at all.
explain: A family can vote for different parties and still go to the match together; no party is all good or all bad, so a post calling one side "evil" and the other "perfect" is cheering, not news.
fact: A political party is a group with shared ideas that tries to win elections; its manifesto is its list of plans and promises.
fact: In the UK, Labour and the Conservatives have been the two biggest parties for a long time.
fact: The two main US parties are the Democrats and the Republicans (also called the GOP); the donkey and the elephant come from old newspaper cartoons.
fact: The left often wants more government spending on shared services and more equality; the right often wants lower taxes, smaller government and tradition; most people hold a mix of views.
fact: Green parties put the environment, such as climate change and nature, first, but have plans on other things too; the UK, Ireland and Australia all have one.
fact: The SNP, the Scottish National Party, wants Scotland to become independent from the UK; in a 2014 referendum most Scottish voters chose to stay, but the debate goes on.
fact: Reform UK is a newer party on the right; it was reported that it rose strongly in opinion polls in 2025; supporters say it speaks for people who feel ignored, critics say its plans are too simple for hard problems.
fact: The Liberal Democrats are a UK party often called "the third party"; in the UK system, smaller parties can win many votes but fewer seats.
fact: A coalition is when two or more parties share running the government; the Conservatives and Liberal Democrats did this in the UK from 2010 to 2015.
fact: An independent is a candidate with no party; independents often win seats in Ireland and Australia.
fact: A whip is the person in a party who tries to make sure its members vote the party way.
fact: A floating voter changes which party they vote for; a loyal voter always votes for the same one.
fact: Partisan means strongly supporting one side; bias means leaning towards one side instead of being fair.
exercise: Dad says Labour and the Conservatives are "like teams". What is a political party? | A group with shared ideas that tries to win elections | A big celebration with cake and music held after every election | A club that only meets in the evening to watch the news | A team of judges in wigs who decide what all the laws say
exercise: What is a manifesto? | A party's list of plans and promises | A map showing where a party's offices are | The final count of votes in an election | A poster that is torn down after voting
exercise: In Ohio, Jake sees an elephant and a donkey on adverts. What do they stand for? | The elephant is Republican, the donkey is Democrat | The elephant is Democrat, the donkey is Republican | They are the mascots of two football teams | They show which animals the zoo will buy
exercise: Zara hears families called "left" and "right". What does it mean? | Rough labels for big ideas about government and taxes | Which hand people use to write and draw | Which side of the road people drive their cars on each day | Which side of the classroom people sit on
exercise: What does the left often want? | The government to do more, like spending on shared services | Taxes as low as possible and smaller government | Every tradition kept exactly as it was | No government services at all
exercise: What does the right often want? | Lower taxes, smaller government and keeping traditions | Much higher taxes for everybody | The government to run every single shop, farm and factory | No elections ever again
exercise: Mia asks if the Greens are only about trees. What is true? | They put the environment first but have plans on other things too | Yes, they only talk about trees and nothing else | They are only against people who cut grass | They are a party for gardeners and nobody else
exercise: Liam's grandad wears an SNP badge. What does the SNP mainly want? | Scotland to become an independent country | Scotland to join the USA as its fifty-first state | Every Scot to move to London | The king to live in Glasgow
exercise: What happened in Scotland's 2014 referendum? | Most voters chose to stay in the UK | Scotland became independent the next day | Nobody was allowed to vote at all | Only children voted, and they said yes
exercise: Finn's uncle cheers Reform UK in a poll and his auntie frowns. What does this show? | People feel strongly both for and against the same party | Everyone in the country agrees about Reform UK | Polls are only about which party has the best logo and colours | Uncles and aunties must vote the same way
exercise: Why do the Lib Dems often come third with many votes? | In the UK system, smaller parties can win many votes but fewer seats | They always ask to come third to be polite | The rules give third place to the oldest party | Their votes are counted only on Sundays
exercise: What is a coalition? | Two or more parties sharing the running of the government | A party that has no candidates at all standing for election | A special type of election held deep down in the coal mines | A vote in which only the party leaders are allowed to take part
exercise: Grace's mum votes for a candidate with no party. What is that candidate called? | An independent | A whip | A coalition | A floating voter
exercise: Oisin's mum and dad vote for different parties. What does this show? | People can disagree about parties and still be close | One of them must be breaking the law | They will have to move into separate houses | Families are only allowed to support one party each by law
exercise: A TikTok says one party is "evil" and the other "perfect". What is the best view? | No party is all good or all bad; hear each side explain itself | It must be true because it was posted online | It is news, because TikTok checks every video | Whichever party it calls perfect must be the best one to vote for
exercise: What is a floating voter? | Someone who changes which party they vote for | Someone who votes while floating in a swimming pool | Someone who never bothers to vote in any election | Someone who votes twice in one day at two places
test: At a sleepover your mate goes, "my fam's left so we're all left-handed, obvi." What's the truth? | Left and right in politics are about ideas, not hands | She's right, left-wing people are all left-handed | She's right, but only on Tuesdays | Left and right are about where people sit in the family car
test: A Glaswegian gran says, "Aye, the SNP, I want Scotland runnin' its own affairs, ken?" ("Ken" means you know.) What is she supporting? | Scottish independence | Scotland joining the USA | Higher bus fares in London | A ban on Scottish elections
test: Your Gen X dad says, "Back in my day the Lib Dems were just the rad underdogs." ("Rad" means great, "underdogs" means the side not expected to win.) What is true about them? | They are a smaller UK party that often comes third | They are the biggest and oldest party in the whole of the USA | They are a party with no ideas at all | They are a club that only accepts dads
test: A cartoon shows the Republican elephant and Democrat donkey as real animals voting. What should be put right? | They are just old cartoon symbols for the two parties | Real elephants and donkeys vote in the USA every four years | Only donkeys are allowed to vote | The animals choose the president by stomping
test: Your cousin says, "Greens = only trees, they've got nothing else, mid." ("Mid" means not very good.) Which is fair? | Greens put the environment first but have plans on other topics too | He's right, Greens only have plans about trees and nothing else at all | Greens are not a real party anywhere | Greens only exist in one tiny village
test: Your uncle is well chuffed (very pleased) about a poll showing Reform UK doing well; your auntie is gutted (very upset). Which is the balanced view? | Supporters say it speaks for people who feel ignored; critics say its plans are too simple | It is the perfect party and everyone should vote for it | It is an evil party and nobody should talk about it | Polls show nothing about what people think
test: What would happen if a country had just one party allowed? | Voters would have no real choice between different ideas | Voters would have more choices than before | Every idea in the country would be heard equally and fairly anyway | Elections would become more exciting to watch
test: A neighbour from the Black Country says, "Ar, our independent's bostin', she knows our area best." ("Ar" means yes; "bostin'" means brilliant.) What is good about an independent? | They answer only to local voters, though they have no party team to help | They always win every vote in Parliament alone | They have the biggest party machine behind them | They never have to listen to voters at all
test: Your brother says, "Floating voters are, like, sus, they can't pick a side." What's a fairer view? | Some say looking at each election fresh is sensible because parties change | He's right, changing your vote is against the law | Floating voters vote in boats, so they are odd | Loyal voters are the only ones allowed to vote
test: Mum says, rather formally, "A coalition government was formed in 2010." What does she mean? | Two parties shared the running of the UK government | One party won every single seat in Parliament that year | The government was run for a while by a big coal company | The election was cancelled and put off for five whole years
test: Your friend shares a post: "Party X is perfect, Party Y is pure evil!!" What is it really? | Partisan cheering, not fair news | A careful, balanced news report | An official result from the count | A message written by the judges
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a political party? | An association of persons sharing common aims who seek election in order to implement them | A ceremonial gathering convened subsequent to an election | A body of judges entrusted with the interpretation of statute | A commercial enterprise engaged in the sale of manifestos
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the terms "left" and "right" in political discourse. | Approximate designations of differing views on the role of government, taxation and tradition | Designations of the hand with which electors customarily write | Designations of the side of the chamber on which children are seated | Precise and fixed categories into which every elector must fall
exam: Ascertain the outcome of the referendum held in Scotland in the year 2014. | A majority of those voting elected to remain within the United Kingdom | A majority of those voting elected to establish an independent state | The referendum was abandoned prior to the count | Participation was restricted to members of the Scottish National Party
exam: Identify the term denoting an arrangement whereby two or more parties jointly conduct the government. | Coalition | Manifesto | Independence | Canvassing
